.TH CHOWN 1 "February 2026" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
chown \- change file owner and group
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B chown
.RB [ \-R ]
.RB [ \-H | \-L | \-P ]
.RB [ \-fhv ]
.I owner[:group]
.I file ...
.RS
.PP
.B chown
.RB [ \-R ]
.RB [ \-H | \-L | \-P ]
.RB [ \-fhv ]
.B \-r
.I file ...
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B chown
utility changes the owner and/or group of each specified
.IR file .
The
.I owner
argument may be a numeric user ID or a login name.
The
.I group
argument may be a numeric group ID or a group name.
When a colon separates owner and group (e.g.,
.IR user :group ),
both are changed.
If only a colon follows the owner, only the group is changed.
.PP
When
.B \-r
is specified,
.B chown
sets the ownership from a reference file (equivalent to
.B \-\-reference
in GNU).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-R
Recursively change the ownership of directories and their contents.
.TP
.B \-H
When used with
.B \-R ,
follow symbolic links that appear on the command line, but do not follow
symbolic links encountered during traversal.
.TP
.B \-L
When used with
.B \-R ,
follow all symbolic links encountered during traversal.
.TP
.B \-P
Do not follow symbolic links during recursive traversal (default).
.TP
.B \-f
Suppress most diagnostics. Errors still cause a nonzero exit status.
.TP
.B \-h
Change the ownership of symbolic links themselves, rather than their targets.
.TP
.B \-r
Change the ownership using a reference file instead of an owner:group operand.
.SH OWNER SPECIFICATION
The
.I owner[:group]
operand specifies the new ownership:
.TP
.I owner
Change owner only.
.TP
.I owner:
Change owner and set group to \-1 (unchanged).
.TP
.I owner:group
Change both owner and group.
.TP
.I :group
Change group only.
.SH ERROR HANDLING
On per\-file failure,
.B chown
records the failure, continues processing remaining operands, and exits
with a nonzero status.
Error messages include the program name and target path unless
.B \-f
is specified.
.SH PORTABILITY NOTES
Setting ownership on symbolic links is not supported on all platforms.
On platforms where it is unsupported,
.B \-h
fails for symbolic links with a clear diagnostic.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All requested ownership changes succeeded.
.TP
.B 1
One or more operations failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B chown user file
Change the owner of
.B file
to
.BR user .
.TP
.B chown user:group file
Change both owner and group of
.BR file .
.TP
.B chown :staff file
Change only the group of
.BR file .
.TP
.B chown -R www:www /var/www
Recursively change owner and group of
.B /var/www
to
.BR www .
.TP
.B chown \-h user /usr/local/bin/link
Change ownership of the symbolic link
.B link
itself, not its target.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR stat (2),
.BR lstat (2),
.BR fchown (2),
.BR chgrp (1),
.BR chmod (1)
